INT. COURTHOUSE - EMPTY COURTROOM 4B - NIGHT

A mid-size municipal court, the kind with fake wood panelling and a drop ceiling. Fluorescent tubes buzz. Half the lights are off, leaving the jury box in gloom and the bench in a pool of white.

Chairs are stacked in the gallery. A cleaning cart stands abandoned by the door, its mop bucket still steaming.

NADIRA SULTANOVA (40s, sharp tailoring gone limp after a fourteen-hour day, hair clipped up with a pen) stands in the well of the court with a legal pad. She faces the empty jury box.

She is alone with the room. Or she thinks she is.

NADIRA
(to the jury box)
Ladies and gentlemen, the state will tell you that my client is a liar.

She waits. Silence. The jury box regards her.

NADIRA (CONT'D)
(to the jury box)
They will tell you he lied about the ledger, about the date, about the signature.

She turns a page.

NADIRA (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Too long. Cut the signature.

She crosses out a line with her pen. Behind her, a soft cough.

She spins. In the back row, half-hidden by the stacked chairs, sits ELIAS THORNQUIST (70s, retired bailiff, grey cleaners' overalls, thick glasses held together with tape). A paperback lies on his knee. He is not reading it.

NADIRA (CONT'D)
(to Elias)
How long have you been there?

ELIAS
Since the second part.

NADIRA
Which part is that?

ELIAS
The part where you said "ledger" like it was a bad word.

Nadira studies him. The tube above her flickers and settles.

NADIRA
I'm rehearsing. The trial starts at nine.

ELIAS
I know. I'll be sitting at the back.

NADIRA
You're a bailiff?

ELIAS
Was. Thirty-one years. Now I clean the brass.

NADIRA
(to the room, dry)
Wonderful. A critic.

Elias grins, a quick crooked flash.

ELIAS
You want a critique?

NADIRA
I want a jury.

ELIAS
Not got one. Got me.

She exhales through her nose. Takes a step toward him, the legal pad hanging at her side.

NADIRA
Fine. Tell me.

ELIAS
You open with the word "liar." Don't.

NADIRA
It's the state's word.

ELIAS
It's still your first word. They'll remember it.

She stops. Absorbs this. Looks back at the jury box as if seeing it for the first time.

NADIRA
(muttering)
He's right.

ELIAS
I'm always right. It's the curse of the cleaning staff.

NADIRA
(to Elias)
What would you open with?

ELIAS
I'd open with the weather.

NADIRA
The weather.

ELIAS
Thirty-one years, I never saw a juror hear a word in the first minute. They're looking for their umbrellas. Give them something to hold. Say it rained that night.

NADIRA
It did rain.

ELIAS
Then say that.

She turns to the empty jury box. Draws a breath. Straightens her spine.

NADIRA
(to the jury box)
It rained on the night of the fourteenth.

Elias nods once. Behind him the mop bucket gives a final hiss.

NADIRA (CONT'D)
(to the jury box)
It rained, and a man went to a bank, and he signed his name.

ELIAS
Better.

NADIRA
(to Elias)
Shut up. I'm working.

He mimes zipping his lips. She almost smiles. She goes on, quieter, rolling each phrase slowly, and he follows her with his eyes, lips moving a half-second behind her words like an old pew regular who has heard the liturgy a thousand times.

She finishes. Silence. The tubes buzz.

ELIAS
(softly)
Ladies and gentlemen.

NADIRA
(to the jury box)
Ladies and gentlemen.

She bows her head. The empty box does not applaud. Elias stands, tucks his paperback under his arm, picks up the mop, and begins on the floor by the door.

NADIRA (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Weather.

FADE OUT.
